Of course, this calculation only represents an approximation of the true average value, since the temperature will vary during each day. 
To determine the true average temperature, we need to know the temperature as a function of time for all instants of time during the one-week period; that is, we consider

	Temperature = T(x), x = elapsed time (in days), for 0 =< x =< 7

Then a more accurate determination of the average temperature is as an integral: 
		Average temperature = 1/7 ∫^(7)(sub(0)) T(x) dx

Since an integra is nothing more than the limit of a sum, it's not hard to see that the preceding formula is a generalization of the original discrete sum calculation to the continuous case. 
